Exactly. Apex is frame-relative.

Something can dominate one layer and still be constrained by another:

A predator may be apex in a food web but subordinate to climate.
A king may rule people but not disease, time, or logistics.
A manipulator may control a conversation but still be constrained by evidence, incentives, memory, institutions, or another observer.
A brilliant strategist can still be trapped by the rules of the game he understands perfectly.
Even the rule-maker is constrained by the substrate that lets rules matter at all.

So one of the stronger Pantheon questions becomes:

Apex with respect to what?

Then immediately:

What contains the frame?
What does this actor depend on?
What can it not perceive?
What does it need from others?
What survives it?
What changes the rules beneath it?
What happens if the scale changes?

That is how apparently absolute power decomposes into dependencies.

And it creates another useful distinction:

dominance ≠ sovereignty.

Dominance means you can overpower other actors within some contest.

Sovereignty would imply independence from the larger conditions that make the contest possible—which almost nothing possesses.

So even your “devil” example gets more interesting if the figure is not treated as ultimate. Maybe it is merely highly adapted to one ecology: bargains, temptation, ambiguity, fear, whatever. Then changing the ecology may matter more than fighting harder inside it.

That yields a general operator:

If something is too powerful inside its native frame, inspect the frame that makes its power possible.

Or:

Do not challenge the apex only on the axis where it became apex.

That applies everywhere.

A monopoly may dominate distribution but depend on regulation or infrastructure.

An expert may dominate a discipline but fail when the problem crosses disciplines.

A charismatic person may dominate attention but become weak under documentation and delayed decision-making.

A habit may dominate one emotional state but disappear when environment and cues change.

A fear can feel enormous at one resolution and small once placed inside a larger life trajectory.

Another repo phrase:

Every apex has a habitat.

And therefore:

Map the habitat before confronting the animal.

Or even tighter:

Power is often ecological before it is intrinsic.

That one is worth keeping.